  "grid_title": "WHAT EACH ONE DOES",
  "why": "Ibuprofen and naproxen are NSAIDs: they block the COX enzymes that make prostaglandins, which drive pain, fever and inflammation, so they ease all three. Naproxen lasts longer, so it's taken less often. Prostaglandins also protect the stomach lining, so NSAIDs can irritate it (and can cause ulcers or bleeding); with heavy or long-term use, or when you're dehydrated, they're hard on the kidneys. Aspirin is also an NSAID, but it blocks platelets' COX-1 for good (the rest of each platelet's life), so blood clots less; some heart patients take a low dose, only on a doctor's advice. Don't give aspirin to children or teenagers with a viral illness like flu or chickenpox: it's linked to Reye's syndrome, which is rare but serious. Acetaminophen (paracetamol) eases pain and fever but does little for swelling; it works mostly in the brain and spinal cord, and exactly how isn't fully understood. It's easier on the stomach, but too much can seriously damage the liver, and it's in many cold and flu combination products, so check every label to avoid doubling up. For a sprain or swelling, an anti-inflammatory can help the swelling too; with stomach problems or blood thinners, ask a pharmacist first. Follow the label, and ask a pharmacist or doctor if you're unsure.",
